WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.500
Defensive TypeScript starts with a simple mindset: values are uncertain until proven otherwise.

2
00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:13.960
Type annotations make expectations visible, but runtime values still need appropriate checks when they cross trust boundaries.

3
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:18.940
Strong systems combine compile-time help with runtime validation where it matters.

4
00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:23.780
Validate at trust boundaries, then keep internal contracts clear.

5
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:30.520
Raw input should be parsed, checked, and converted into safer internal shapes before business logic depends on it.

6
00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:37.180
Once data has been validated, the rest of the system can operate with fewer repeated checks and more meaningful assumptions.

7
00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:41.220
Use the type system to make invalid states harder to represent.

8
00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:54.680
Prefer unknown over any at boundaries, model workflows with discriminated unions, enable strict settings where practical, use readonly contracts when mutation is not intended, and handle cases exhaustively so future changes are visible.

9
00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:03.840
Design error handling, async behavior, configuration, dependencies, authorization, serialization, and data flow intentionally.

10
00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:08.420
Defensive code treats operational failure as normal engineering input.

11
00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:18.280
It uses timeouts, careful retries, safe logging, startup configuration validation, minimal data exposure, and clear permission checks near protected actions.

12
00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:21.120
Prefer safe failure over surprising success.

13
00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:29.300
A system that rejects bad input clearly is easier to operate than a system that accepts almost anything and fails later.

14
00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:32.920
Defensive programming is not about adding noise.

15
00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:45.020
It is about making important assumptions visible, testable, and maintainable so TypeScript applications remain reliable as teams, dependencies, and runtime conditions change.
